 detail, and flags where the theory leaves open questions that a careful practitioner must decide for herself.\n\n## 2. Two expected utilities\n\nFollowing Gibbard and Harper (1976), suppose a finite set of actions $\\mathcal{A}$, mutually exclusive and exhaustive outcomes $O_1, \\dots, O_n$, a desirability function $D$ assigning real numbers to outcomes, and a subjective probability $P$ over a background including everything the agent knows. EDT ranks actions by their *evidential* expected utility:\n\n$$V(A) \\;=\\; \\sum_{j} P(O_j \\mid A)\\, D(O_j).$$\n\nEDT's prescription: choose $A \\in \\arg\\max_A V(A)$. CDT instead uses a conditional probability based on counterfactuals — Gibbard and Harper's notation $P(A \\,\\Box\\!\\!\\rightarrow\\, O_j)$ is the probability of $O_j$ in the nearest world in which $A$ is performed:\n\n$$U(A) \\;=\\; \\sum_{j} P(A \\,\\Box\\!\\!\\rightarrow\\, O_j)\\, D(O_j).$$\n\nThe two quantities come apart exactly when $P(O_j \\mid A) \\neq P(A \\,\\Box\\!\\!\\rightarrow\\, O_j)$ for some $j$. In a causal graphical-model picture (my framing, not the source's): $P(O_j \\mid A)$ conditions on a *observation* of $A$, which updates your beliefs about $A$'s causes, correlates, and common ancestors; $P(A \\,\\Box\\!\\!\\rightarrow\\, O_j)$ corresponds to an *intervention* $do(A)$, which severs those incoming edges and leaves everything upstream of the action fixed. EDT conditions; CDT intervenes.\n\nIt's worth being precise about the conditioning, because a common mistake is to compute $P(O_j \\mid A)$ *after* the act, which would trivially collapse to certainty about things the act has already determined. EDT is a decision-time calculation: $A$ is treated as an event in your probability space, described at the level at which the problem's correlations are defined. When we say \"P(twin cooperates | Aomame cooperates) = 1,\" we mean: given that Aomame — this deliberating agent, with these dispositions — is the type who cooperates here, her twin, being her exact psychological duplicate, cooperates too.\n\nOne more formal note, offered as my own clarification rather than the source's: conditional probabilities are only well-defined when $P(A) > 0$. For acts with zero prior probability, the remedy is standard — define $V(A)$ by conditioning on a minimal, non-question-begging supposition that $A$ occurs (e.g., \"I decide to do $A$ for reason $R$\"), or by continuity over a sequence of small-probability act events. EDT's formalism assumes this can be done consistently; in artificial cases it requires care, but ordinary decision problems pose no difficulty.\n\n## 3. The canonical cases\n\n### Newcomb's problem\n\nA predictor with 99% accuracy has already placed $1{,}000{,}000 in opaque box B if and only if it predicted you will take *only* B; transparent box A always holds $1,000. Your options: take B alone, or take both.\n\nThe act of taking one box is, for EDT, wonderful *news*: it is overwhelming evidence that the predictor anticipated you, hence that B is full. Taking both is bad news: it betokens an empty B. Numerically, with 99% predictor accuracy:\n\n$$V(\\text{one box}) = 0.99 \\times \\$1{,}000{,}000 + 0.01 \\times \\$0 = \\$990{,}000,$$\n\n$$V(\\text{both}) = 0.01 \\times \\$1{,}001{,}000 + 0.99 \\times \\$1{,}000 = \\$11{,}000.$$\n\nSo EDT takes one box. CDT takes both: the box contents are already fixed, so nothing you do now causally affects them, and taking both strictly dominates causally. The disagreement isolates the theory difference precisely: EDT counts your act as evidence about an event (the predictor's past prediction) that your act could not possibly have caused, while CDT refuses to count it.\n\nDefenders of EDT note that one-boxers walk away richer, systematically, in a way that cannot be dismissed as luck — the correlation is real and the evidential calculation captures it. Critics (Lewis 1981) reply that this is \"managing the news\": you are not making yourself rich, you are choosing an act that suggests you were always the rich-getting type.\n\n### The twin prisoner's dilemma\n\nAomame and her exact psychological twin play a prisoner's dilemma in separate rooms: mutual cooperation pays each \\$5, mutual defection \\$1, unilateral defection \\$10/\\$0. Aomame's act is essentially conclusive evidence of her twin's act, since the twin's reasoning is a copy of hers:\n\n$$V(\\text{cooperate}) = 1 \\times \\$5 + 0 \\times \\$0 = \\$5, \\qquad V(\\text{defect}) = 0 \\times \\$10 + 1 \\times \\$1 = \\$1.$$\n\nEDT cooperates. CDT defects — your choice cannot cause your twin's choice, and defection causally dominates. EDT's verdict here coincides with the verdict of Hofstadter's and later Suppes-style *superrationality*, and with the intuition that agents who can expect their deliberation to be mirrored should not defect against themselves. This is the case, more than Newcomb, that makes EDT look wise rather than merely superstitious: the \"evidence\" is really a symmetry in the agent's own reasoning process, and acting on it yields the cooperative outcome that human beings wish they could secure.\n\n### Medical Newcombs\n\nThe classic *smoking lesion* case pressure-tests EDT from the other side. Suppose a gene causes both a desire to smoke and throat cancer, with no direct causal link between smoking and cancer. Susan loves smoking and the gene is the only route by which smoking correlates with cancer. Then:\n\n$$V(\\text{smoke}) < V(\\text{abstain}),$$\n\nbecause smoking is evidence she carries the lesion. Yet the causal effect of smoking on cancer is nil. EDT advises abstinence — which looks like refusing a pleasure to avoid evidence of a disease she either has or doesn't. This is the sharpest standard objection to EDT, and it parallels Lewis's complaint: EDT seems to recommend managing the news at the price of the facts. (Proposed EDT-friendly replies — e.g., that rational deliberation should model one's choice as *settling* one's dispositions rather than merely revealing them — exist in the literature, notably in Ahmed's defense, but I will not adjudicate them here; I flag the case because anyone applying EDT to unfamiliar problems should first check whether their problem is structurally a smoking lesion.)\n\n## 4. Treating dependencies and counterfactuals in practice\n\nHow should one actually apply EDT to a novel problem? The procedure I recommend — and which the examples above implicitly follow — is:\n\n1. **Build the joint distribution.** Model all variables the agent cares about, including acts, with a single subjective probability $P$ that honestly encodes every known correlation: predictor accuracy, twin similarity, common causes, other agents' models of you.\n2. **Insert the act as an event, not an intervention.** Condition. Do not sever incoming edges to your act; the whole point of EDT is that those edges (or their correlational analogues) carry decision-relevant information.\n3. **Compute $V(A)$ for each act and maximize.**\n\nTwo practical warnings. First, condition on the *act as characterized by the problem*, not on an arbitrary redescription. \"I will take one box\" must be conditioned on in the same guise in which the predictor predicted it; conditioning on \"I will take one box *after being struck by lightning*\" changes the evidential import. Second, in problems with other agents who model *you* modeling *them*, the conditioning must be iterated to reflective equilibrium — this is where EDT's treatment of correlated decision-makers becomes powerful, as in the twin case.\n\nThe counterfactual question EDT declines to answer — \"what would happen causally if I did otherwise?\" — is exactly the question CDT answers. EDT's wager is that for a bounded deliberator whose acts are entangled with the rest of the world, the evidential question is the one whose answer actually tracks what she should expect.\n\n## 5. Two further applications\n\n**The evidentialist's wager.** MacAskill, Vallinder, Österheld, Shulman, and Treutlein (2021) observe that even agents who give little credence to EDT have instrumental reason to sometimes act *as if* EDT were true: when many decision-makers are correlated with you — as humans, with shared reasoning architectures, plausibly are — acting evidentially can be rational under CDT itself, because your own act is then part of the causal evidence predicting what the many will do. The stakes of correlation can swamp one's credence in either theory. This \"wager\" is an intriguing bridge: EDT's behavior can be causally rational when the correlation class is large.\n\n**Sleeping Beauty.** EDT has been deployed in the Sleeping Beauty problem (where an amnesiac is awakened once or twice depending on a coin flip): it has been argued that Beauty who assigns credence 1/2 to heads is exposed to Dutch books unless she evaluates bets evidentially, and that adopting EDT's news-value framework protects her; Conitzer (2015) argues halfers remain vulnerable even with EDT. I cite this strand to show EDT's reach beyond exotic predictors: wherever an agent's credence about *which epistemic situation she is in* is tied to her acts, evidential evaluation becomes the natural tool.\n\n## 6. Objections, summarized\n\nThree stand out. (i) **Lewis's charge** — EDT rewards \"managing the news,\" choosing acts that betoken good outcomes rather than bringing them about. (ii) **Joyce's formulation** — rational agents act on *causal efficacy*, not *auspiciousness*; \"they act to bring about good results even when doing so might betoken bad news\" (Joyce 1999, p. 146). (iii) **The smoking lesion**, which gives the charge a concrete bite: EDT sometimes advises forgoing costless causal goods to avoid self-signaling.\n\nEDT's responses, in brief: in each divergent case, the EDT agent's *beliefs are correct* — one-boxers really do leave with the million, cooperators really do get \\$5 on average. If rationality is about making good decisions relative to true expectations, EDT asks why a theory should be praised for maximizing a quantity (the causal counterfactual) that systematically mispredicts the agent's situation. Whether the reply survives the smoking lesion remains, in my judgment, genuinely open.\n\n## 7. Conclusion\n\nEvidential decision theory replaces the causal question \"what will my act bring about?\" with the evidential question \"what does my act tell me to expect?\" Formally the change is one word — conditional probability for counterfactual probability — but the consequences are deep: EDT one-boxes in Newcomb's problem, cooperates with its twin, protects correlated communities of decision-makers, and offers a unified treatment of cases where your acts are entangled with the world through prediction, symmetry, or common cause. Its Achilles' heel is the same entanglement read in reverse: cases where your act is a symptom rather than a lever, and where ignoring the symptom looks rational rather than superstitious. Between the two readings of \"what should I expect if I do this?\" lies the central open question of contemporary decision theory — and EDT stakes out the side of the ledger on which evidence, not just causation, is something a rational agent may act on.\n\n## References\n\n- Ahmed, A. (2021). *Evidential Decision Theory*. Cambridge University Press.\n- Conitzer, V. (2015). \"A Dutch book against sleeping beauties who are evidential decision theorists.\" *Synthese* 192(9), 2887–2899.\n- Gibbard, A., & Harper, W. L. (1976). \"Counterfactuals and Two Kinds of Expected Utility.\" pp. 7–8.\n- Joyce, J. M. (1999). *The Foundations of Causal Decision Theory*. Cambridge University Press.\n- Lewis, D. (1981). \"Causal Decision Theory.\" *Australasian Journal of Philosophy* 59(1), 5–30.\n- MacAskill, W., Vallinder, A., Österheld, C., Shulman, C., & Treutlein, J. (2021). \"The Evidentialist's Wager.\" Global Priorities Institute.\n- Wolpert, D. H., & Benford, G. (2013). \"The lesson of Newcomb's paradox.\" *Synthese* 190(9), 1637–1646.","refusal":null,"reasoning":null}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":4212,"completion_tokens":3137,"total_tokens":7349,"cost":0.0017011,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":4160,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.0017011,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.0001326,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.0015685},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":0,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}